ref_completion:
To determine where the rotation takes the vector \(\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}\), we first need to understand the properties of the rotation. A rotation in the plane about the origin can be represented by a matrix of the form:

\[
R = \begin{pmatrix}
\cos \theta & -\sin \theta \\
\sin \theta & \cos \theta
\end{pmatrix}
\]

where \(\theta\) is the angle of rotation.

Given that the vector \(\begin{pmatrix} 13 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}\) is rotated to \(\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ -12 \end{pmatrix}\), we can use this information to find the angle \(\theta\). The original vector \(\begin{pmatrix} 13 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}\) lies along the positive x-axis. After rotation, it becomes \(\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ -12 \end{pmatrix}\). The cosine and sine of the angle \(\theta\) can be determined from these coordinates because:

\[
\cos \theta = \frac{5}{13}, \quad \sin \theta = -\frac{12}{13}
\]

Now, we apply this rotation matrix to the vector \(\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}\):

\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos \theta & -\sin \theta \\
\sin \theta & \cos \theta
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos \theta \cdot 0 + (-\sin \theta) \cdot 1 \\
\sin \theta \cdot 0 + \cos \theta \cdot 1
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
-\sin \theta \\
\cos \theta
\end{pmatrix}
\]

Substituting the values of \(\cos \theta\) and \(\sin \theta\):

\[
\begin{pmatrix}
-(-\frac{12}{13}) \\
\frac{5}{13}
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
\frac{12}{13} \\
\frac{5}{13}
\end{pmatrix}
\]

Thus, the vector \(\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}\) is taken to \(\begin{pmatrix} \frac{12}{13} \\ \frac{5}{13} \end{pmatrix}\).

\(\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} \frac{12}{13} \\ \frac{5}{13} \end{pmatrix}}\)

