We want to simplify $\tan 9^\circ + \cot 9^\circ - \tan 27^\circ - \cot 27^\circ$. We can rewrite this expression as $(\tan 9^\circ - \tan 27^\circ) + (\cot 9^\circ - \cot 27^\circ)$.
Recall that $\tan x = \frac{\sin x}{\cos x}$ and $\cot x = \frac{\cos x}{\sin x}$. Then we have
\begin{align*} \label{eq:1}\tan 9^\circ - \tan 27^\circ &= \frac{\sin 9^\circ}{\cos 9^\circ} - \frac{\sin 27^\circ}{\cos 27^\circ} \\ &= \frac{\sin 9^\circ \cos 27^\circ - \cos 9^\circ \sin 27^\circ}{\cos 9^\circ \cos 27^\circ} \\ &= \frac{\sin(9^\circ - 27^\circ)}{\cos 9^\circ \cos 27^\circ} \\ &= \frac{\sin(-18^\circ)}{\cos 9^\circ \cos 27^\circ} \\ &= -\frac{\sin 18^\circ}{\cos 9^\circ \cos 27^\circ}\end{aligned}
and
\begin{align*}\cot 9^\circ - \cot 27^\circ &= \frac{\cos 9^\circ}{\sin 9^\circ} - \frac{\cos 27^\circ}{\sin 27^\circ} \\ &= \frac{\cos 9^\circ \sin 27^\circ - \sin 9^\circ \cos 27^\circ}{\sin 9^\circ \sin 27^\circ} \\ &= \frac{\sin(27^\circ - 9^\circ)}{\sin 9^\circ \sin 27^\circ} \\ &= \frac{\sin 18^\circ}{\sin 9^\circ \sin 27^\circ}.\end{align*}
Therefore,
\begin{align*} \tan 9^\circ + \cot 9^\circ - \tan 27^\circ - \cot 27^\circ &= -\frac{\sin 18^\circ}{\cos 9^\circ \cos 27^\circ} + \frac{\sin 18^\circ}{\sin 9^\circ \sin 27^\circ} \\ &= \sin 18^\circ \left(\frac{1}{\sin 9^\circ \sin 27^\circ} - \frac{1}{\cos 9^\circ \cos 27^\circ}\right) \\ &= \sin 18^\circ \left(\frac{\cos 9^\circ \cos 27^\circ - \sin 9^\circ \sin 27^\circ}{\sin 9^\circ \sin 27^\circ \cos 9^\circ \cos 27^\circ}\right) \\ &= \sin 18^\circ \left(\frac{\cos(9^\circ+27^\circ)}{\sin 9^\circ \sin 27^\circ \cos 9^\circ \cos 27^\circ}\right) \\ &= \sin 18^\circ \left(\frac{\cos 36^\circ}{\sin 9^\circ \sin 27^\circ \cos 9^\circ \cos 27^\circ}\right) \\ &= \sin 18^\circ \left(\frac{\cos 36^\circ}{\frac{1}{2}(\sin 18^\circ)(\sin 54^\circ)}\right) \\ &= \sin 18^\circ \left(\frac{\cos 36^\circ}{\frac{1}{2} \sin 18^\circ \cos 36^\circ}\right) \\ &= \frac{\sin 18^\circ \cos 36^\circ}{\frac{1}{2} \sin 18^\circ \cos 36^\circ} \\ &= 2.\end{align*}
Here we used the fact that $\sin 18^\circ = \frac{\sqrt{5}-1}{4}$, $\cos 36^\circ = \frac{\sqrt{5}+1}{4}$, $\sin 27^\circ = \sin(30^\circ - 3^\circ)$ and $\cos 27^\circ = \cos(30^\circ - 3^\circ)$, but these values were not needed. Also, we used the identity $2 \sin A \cos B = \sin(A+B) + \sin(A-B)$.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{2}$

Let $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}$. We want to find the matrix $A$ such that $A\mathbf{x}$ reflects $\mathbf{x}$ across the line spanned by $\mathbf{v}$. The formula for reflection across a vector $\mathbf{v}$ is given by
\[ A = I - 2 \operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf{v}} = I - 2 \frac{\mathbf{v}\mathbf{v}^T}{\|\mathbf{v}\|^2}. \]Here,
\[ \mathbf{v}\mathbf{v}^T = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} -1 & 3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & -3 \\ -3 & 9 \end{pmatrix}, \]and $\|\mathbf{v}\|^2 = (-1)^2 + 3^2 = 10$. Therefore,
\begin{align*} A &= I - 2 \frac{\mathbf{v}\mathbf{v}^T}{\|\mathbf{v}\|^2} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} - 2 \frac{1}{10} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & -3 \\ -3 & 9 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} - \frac{1}{5} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & -3 \\ -3 & 9 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 1/5 & -3/5 \\ -3/5 & 9/5 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 1 - 1/5 & 0 - (-3/5) \\ 0 - (-3/5) & 1 - 9/5 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 4/5 & 3/5 \\ 3/5 & -4/5 \end{pmatrix}. \end{align*}

\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 4/5 & 3/5 \\ 3/5 & -4/5 \end{pmatrix}}
